1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,580
The next time you go out into the wilderness and you're up there in the mountains, I encourage

2
00:00:14,580 --> 00:00:20,300
you to sit still and look at the vista before you and listen carefully because you can

3
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:23,400
hear the mountains speak.

4
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:27,200
Honestly, you can.

5
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:31,960
They talk about a range of topics.

6
00:00:31,960 --> 00:00:34,960
A range of topics, mountains.

7
00:00:34,960 --> 00:00:35,960
Yeah.

8
00:00:35,960 --> 00:00:36,960
Oh my go-well.

9
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:39,960
Hey, this is podcast number 59.

10
00:00:57,200 --> 00:01:11,080
Welcome back to the Scoutmaster Podcast.

11
00:01:11,080 --> 00:01:13,400
This is Clark Green.

12
00:01:13,400 --> 00:01:15,840
So let's take a look at the email bag here.

13
00:01:15,840 --> 00:01:21,080
Actually, it's not a bag, but you know, sounds good, right?

14
00:01:21,080 --> 00:01:24,480
Bob Martin wrote this, thanks a million Clark, I did a survey at the end of the

15
00:01:24,480 --> 00:01:33,840
day and your session averaged 4.5 out of 5 slightly ahead of lunch.

16
00:01:33,840 --> 00:01:36,360
It's always good to be slightly ahead of lunch.

17
00:01:36,360 --> 00:01:42,680
That was from a virtual appearance that I did in Murfreesboro, Tennessee.

18
00:01:42,680 --> 00:01:48,760
Bob Martin, thanks again for helping out and making that happen.

19
00:01:48,760 --> 00:01:50,440
We had a great time.

20
00:01:50,600 --> 00:01:57,200
Tom Gillard was at the conference and he wrote this, I heard from you yesterday.

21
00:01:57,200 --> 00:02:03,920
I'm going to enjoy catching up with you on your webpage and listening to the podcast.

22
00:02:03,920 --> 00:02:06,600
Thanks again for the work that you put into your blog and podcast.

23
00:02:06,600 --> 00:02:09,360
I'm enjoying it as corny as the jokes are.

24
00:02:09,360 --> 00:02:12,020
I do laugh at them.

25
00:02:12,020 --> 00:02:13,920
Maybe just a little chuckle.

26
00:02:13,920 --> 00:02:16,760
Hey, you know, better than nothing, right?

27
00:02:16,760 --> 00:02:23,120
Thanks again for all the work you're doing to help us and help us help the boys.

28
00:02:23,120 --> 00:02:25,280
I got this announcement this week.

29
00:02:25,280 --> 00:02:31,880
If you're taking a trip into the Boundary Waters canoe area this summer or you're

30
00:02:31,880 --> 00:02:37,359
going on a river paddling adventure or dreaming of an extended wilderness trip,

31
00:02:37,359 --> 00:02:41,799
maybe you need a refresher covering the fundamentals or something to sharpen your

32
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:43,760
backcountry skills.

33
00:02:43,760 --> 00:02:50,160
And Cliff Jacobson, who was interviewed a couple of podcasts ago, legendary paddler

34
00:02:50,160 --> 00:02:57,200
and guide and author, donated a weekend of his time to conduct a special workshop.

35
00:02:57,200 --> 00:03:02,160
And all the proceeds from this workshop are going to go to Northern Star Council's

36
00:03:02,160 --> 00:03:05,480
Eagle Scout Scholarship Fund.

37
00:03:05,480 --> 00:03:12,079
And it provides post-secondary scholarships for 25 Northern Star Council Eagle Scouts.

38
00:03:12,079 --> 00:03:17,519
This is going to take place at the Fred C. Anderson Scout Camp in Houlton, Wisconsin,

39
00:03:17,519 --> 00:03:22,880
which is just outside of Stillwater, Minnesota, from April 1st to the 3rd.

40
00:03:22,880 --> 00:03:30,399
There will be a link in the post that contains this podcast at scoutmaster.typepad.com

41
00:03:30,399 --> 00:03:34,320
so that you can find out more information about this.

42
00:03:34,359 --> 00:03:40,120
Cliff is a distinguished Eagle Scout and is offering his time to do this and it's

43
00:03:40,120 --> 00:03:45,480
a pretty modest cost and it looks great and if I was any closer to Wisconsin I would

44
00:03:45,480 --> 00:03:46,959
be there.

45
00:03:46,959 --> 00:03:52,240
But just in case you're in the area and this appeals to you, and it should, follow

46
00:03:52,240 --> 00:03:56,079
the link in the post that contains this podcast.

47
00:03:56,079 --> 00:04:03,560
The newsletter went out in February and we had our monthly giveaway contest.

48
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:07,800
This month's giveaway winner is Dave Klein.

49
00:04:07,800 --> 00:04:12,080
And Dave is from Lake Forest in Orange County, California.

50
00:04:12,080 --> 00:04:16,800
He's an assistant scoutmaster and he's been involved in scouting for about 7 years

51
00:04:16,800 --> 00:04:21,720
and he has an 8-year-old and a 13-year-old scout at home and he listens to the podcast

52
00:04:21,720 --> 00:04:25,680
on his lunch break at work and he says it's better for his blood pressure than

53
00:04:25,680 --> 00:04:27,360
political talk radio.

54
00:04:27,360 --> 00:04:33,400
Well yeah, I guess the joke part might send some people's blood pressure up but

55
00:04:33,440 --> 00:04:34,800
you know, oh wow.

56
00:04:34,800 --> 00:04:40,520
Dave answered a trivia question to win four of Nalgene's newest water bottles.

57
00:04:40,520 --> 00:04:44,560
Yeah, with some really cool features and we've talked about those on the blog

58
00:04:44,560 --> 00:04:48,080
and the podcast and he will be receiving those.

59
00:04:48,080 --> 00:04:54,160
The trivia question Dave answered was, who was the author of the original

60
00:04:54,160 --> 00:04:56,640
handbook for patrol leaders?

61
00:04:56,640 --> 00:05:00,280
Who was the author of the original handbook for patrol leaders?

62
00:05:00,360 --> 00:05:07,360
And the correct answer was William Greenbar Bill Hillcourt.

63
00:05:07,360 --> 00:05:09,040
Bill Hillcourt.

64
00:05:09,040 --> 00:05:11,640
You can subscribe to the Scoutmaster newsletter.

65
00:05:11,640 --> 00:05:17,240
Go to www.scoutmaster.typed.com and you'll see a link to subscribe to the newsletter

66
00:05:17,240 --> 00:05:22,560
and there's always a great monthly giveaway and the giveaway for this coming newsletter

67
00:05:22,560 --> 00:05:26,080
are products from E-Lite, E-Lite.

68
00:05:26,080 --> 00:05:31,680
You might have heard E-Lite mentioned in the interview I did a podcast or two ago with

69
00:05:31,680 --> 00:05:37,000
Dr. Paul Auerbach and we'll talk a little bit more about that in the newsletter and

70
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:41,280
you'll see a review of those products on the blog very soon.

71
00:05:41,280 --> 00:05:45,360
Speaking of Dr. Paul, Frank Maynard wrote in to say he enjoyed the interview in

72
00:05:45,360 --> 00:05:48,520
podcast number 57 with Dr. Auerbach.

73
00:05:48,520 --> 00:05:53,320
Tracy wrote in to say some of our guys never miss an episode of Survivorman or

74
00:05:53,320 --> 00:05:55,080
Man vs. Wild.

75
00:05:55,560 --> 00:06:01,840
Thanks for your help and thanks for the highlight at the beginning of my every week this blog.

76
00:06:01,840 --> 00:06:04,560
Well thank you Tracy, certainly do appreciate it.

77
00:06:04,560 --> 00:06:09,719
We had an interview last week with Les Stroud, the Survivorman guy.

78
00:06:09,719 --> 00:06:14,320
This week we've got lots of interesting things to do in Scoutmastership in seven

79
00:06:14,320 --> 00:06:15,520
minutes or less.

80
00:06:15,520 --> 00:06:18,360
We're going to be talking about mental health.

81
00:06:18,360 --> 00:06:22,159
Not your mental health, not my mental health because you know that's a foregone conclusion

82
00:06:22,200 --> 00:06:24,200
I think.

83
00:06:24,200 --> 00:06:28,520
Yeah, but we're going to talk about mental health awareness in Scouting and

84
00:06:28,520 --> 00:06:35,280
then we have a telephone call that came in about what are we going to do to

85
00:06:35,280 --> 00:06:43,160
make moms feel safe and confident about their first year Scouts going to

86
00:06:43,160 --> 00:06:44,680
summer camp.

87
00:06:44,680 --> 00:06:45,840
That's a big one.

88
00:06:45,840 --> 00:06:48,240
And that is plenty to do for this podcast.

89
00:06:48,240 --> 00:06:49,520
So let's get started, shall we?

90
00:06:49,520 --> 00:06:54,080
Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

91
00:07:02,080 --> 00:07:09,840
Several years ago, I missed a backpacking trip and I had a couple of job related

92
00:07:09,840 --> 00:07:13,159
things I had to do and there was just no way out of it.

93
00:07:13,159 --> 00:07:17,359
So I sent the Scouts off backpacking and then the following Monday night at a

94
00:07:17,360 --> 00:07:22,320
troop meeting, I was approached by a very upset parent whose son had been

95
00:07:22,320 --> 00:07:27,240
physically attacked by another Scout during the outing.

96
00:07:27,240 --> 00:07:28,880
This was not good.

97
00:07:28,880 --> 00:07:30,840
We have these moments.

98
00:07:30,840 --> 00:07:35,800
So I spoke with both the Scouts and you know, it was clear that one of

99
00:07:35,800 --> 00:07:39,960
them was mouthing off and really pestering the other one and the other

100
00:07:39,960 --> 00:07:44,480
one decided that he was going to react physically.

101
00:07:44,480 --> 00:07:48,120
Now, both of these guys obviously needed to modify their behavior.

102
00:07:48,120 --> 00:07:50,520
Both of them were acting poorly.

103
00:07:50,520 --> 00:07:56,600
But one thing I was really concerned about was the Scout who got physical

104
00:07:56,600 --> 00:07:58,200
and attacked the other one.

105
00:07:58,200 --> 00:08:03,720
This was like the second time that he'd been part of a fight.

106
00:08:03,720 --> 00:08:08,560
He was, I say was, because he went on to become an Eagle Scout and he's

107
00:08:08,560 --> 00:08:12,640
off to college now and you know, doing very well.

108
00:08:12,640 --> 00:08:18,400
He just, he had some real signs of being unable to control his actions in

109
00:08:18,400 --> 00:08:20,520
that kind of a situation.

110
00:08:20,520 --> 00:08:23,960
And then after I talked to him, worse yet, you know, he felt perfectly

111
00:08:23,960 --> 00:08:29,400
justified in what he had done and he was ready to do it again in the

112
00:08:29,400 --> 00:08:31,240
same circumstances.

113
00:08:31,240 --> 00:08:37,679
Now this was an intelligent young man and mature enough to know that this

114
00:08:37,679 --> 00:08:40,640
was probably not a good way to handle things.

115
00:08:40,679 --> 00:08:45,280
Yet he was angry enough that it caused him to see it as the only course of

116
00:08:45,280 --> 00:08:47,840
action he had left.

117
00:08:47,840 --> 00:08:55,720
Now, I sat and talked with his dad and I told him that I wouldn't allow

118
00:08:55,720 --> 00:09:01,000
him to participate in any other Scout functions until we were sure

119
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:03,800
that things were okay.

120
00:09:03,800 --> 00:09:08,000
And I suggested that the best way to do that would be to get a little

121
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:08,960
counseling.

122
00:09:08,960 --> 00:09:11,720
Now dad was not happy with this.

123
00:09:11,720 --> 00:09:15,440
You know, predictably he felt I was overreacting to this kind of

124
00:09:15,440 --> 00:09:18,520
boys will be boys incident.

125
00:09:18,520 --> 00:09:23,760
But my decision in doing this wasn't based on finding fault.

126
00:09:23,760 --> 00:09:28,120
It was based on my concern that this boy could probably use a little

127
00:09:28,120 --> 00:09:32,960
assistance in sorting out what was causing his problems.

128
00:09:32,960 --> 00:09:36,800
He was miserable and he was conflicted and he was clearly having

129
00:09:36,800 --> 00:09:38,320
some difficulties.

130
00:09:38,320 --> 00:09:41,880
And I got to tell you, frankly I was afraid for him.

131
00:09:41,880 --> 00:09:48,360
In the end his dad agreed and he went to do a session or two with a

132
00:09:48,360 --> 00:09:53,240
counselor and the counselor reported they were successful in assisting the

133
00:09:53,240 --> 00:09:57,480
Scout and things were pretty much back to normal.

134
00:09:57,480 --> 00:10:00,480
And both of the boys that were involved in the incident managed to

135
00:10:00,480 --> 00:10:04,560
get along and both went on to become Eagles.

136
00:10:04,560 --> 00:10:07,840
This might be a pretty familiar story to a lot of you.

137
00:10:07,840 --> 00:10:09,920
You've probably encountered something like this.

138
00:10:09,920 --> 00:10:13,120
If you've been around for enough years this is going to happen.

139
00:10:13,120 --> 00:10:18,840
And every so often we hear of some horrible unspeakable tragedy that

140
00:10:18,840 --> 00:10:21,400
ends the life of a young person.

141
00:10:21,400 --> 00:10:25,960
And in the aftermath we see signs that led to the tragedy and we

142
00:10:25,960 --> 00:10:28,360
just wonder why nobody noticed.

143
00:10:28,360 --> 00:10:31,880
It's not one of the best parts of being a Scout leader.

144
00:10:31,880 --> 00:10:35,040
It's not one of the fun parts of being a Scout leader.

145
00:10:35,040 --> 00:10:37,959
But we have to notice these things sometimes.

146
00:10:37,959 --> 00:10:41,920
And we have to know how to react and what to do.

147
00:10:41,920 --> 00:10:44,800
So I would suggest this to you.

148
00:10:44,800 --> 00:10:49,760
I found some good resources at the National Mental Health Information

149
00:10:49,760 --> 00:10:50,560
Center.

150
00:10:50,560 --> 00:10:52,439
It's a real good place to start.

151
00:10:52,439 --> 00:10:57,520
And I've made up a fact sheet from their Child and Adolescent Mental

152
00:10:57,520 --> 00:10:58,920
Health section.

153
00:10:58,920 --> 00:11:03,439
And as you read you'll see that there are some warning signs listed

154
00:11:03,440 --> 00:11:07,400
and some of these are pretty common things that you see in adolescence.

155
00:11:07,400 --> 00:11:13,040
We pay attention because these signs may point to mental health

156
00:11:13,040 --> 00:11:14,240
disorders.

157
00:11:14,240 --> 00:11:18,200
It's not that each thing is a definitive indicator of a mental

158
00:11:18,200 --> 00:11:21,000
illness or a serious disorder.

159
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:24,920
But every once in a while a young person needs a little help and they

160
00:11:24,920 --> 00:11:28,960
need to sit and talk and they need to work with a counselor.

161
00:11:28,960 --> 00:11:31,240
I'm not a professional counselor.

162
00:11:31,240 --> 00:11:32,880
I'm not.

163
00:11:32,880 --> 00:11:36,720
If you have a real nice pocket watch you don't take the back off the watch and

164
00:11:36,720 --> 00:11:41,480
start messing around with the guts of the watch because you know if you've

165
00:11:41,480 --> 00:11:47,120
tried that I've tried that you can you can screw things up.

166
00:11:47,120 --> 00:11:51,920
It's very delicately balanced instrument and people's people's minds

167
00:11:51,920 --> 00:11:57,040
very delicately balanced instruments and you got to know what you're doing.

168
00:11:57,040 --> 00:12:01,200
So let me go into this fact sheet a little bit and just give you a little

169
00:12:01,200 --> 00:12:02,200
summary of it.

170
00:12:02,240 --> 00:12:07,040
And there'll be a link to it in the post that contains this podcast on the

171
00:12:07,040 --> 00:12:08,040
blog.

172
00:12:09,480 --> 00:12:12,080
One of the things that says it says this like adults children and

173
00:12:12,080 --> 00:12:15,360
adolescents can have mental health disorders that interfere with the way

174
00:12:15,360 --> 00:12:19,280
they think feel and act when untreated mental health disorders can

175
00:12:19,280 --> 00:12:23,320
lead to school failure family conflicts drug abuse violence and even

176
00:12:23,320 --> 00:12:24,360
suicide.

177
00:12:24,360 --> 00:12:28,800
Now listen we're being realistic here correct.

178
00:12:28,800 --> 00:12:34,240
I mean just because we see some things in adolescent boys that you know are a

179
00:12:34,240 --> 00:12:40,360
little outside the norm and are an expression of their journey into

180
00:12:40,360 --> 00:12:44,560
adulthood it doesn't necessarily mean that every single thing we see is a

181
00:12:44,560 --> 00:12:50,160
cause for concern but there are signs and when you see them in

182
00:12:50,160 --> 00:12:54,240
combinations or you have a sense that things aren't going well it's a

183
00:12:54,240 --> 00:13:00,360
good idea to mention it to talk maybe have a little discussion with a set of

184
00:13:00,360 --> 00:13:04,240
parents and say you know I've noticed these things and and and you've

185
00:13:04,240 --> 00:13:05,880
probably noticed them too.

186
00:13:05,880 --> 00:13:09,040
I just want to make sure that you know it's a it's an area of concern

187
00:13:09,040 --> 00:13:13,960
for me and whether or not there's anything I can do to help you with it.

188
00:13:13,960 --> 00:13:18,120
The resource that I'm offering you lists a number of signs a child or

189
00:13:18,120 --> 00:13:22,640
adolescent might be troubled by feeling sad and hopeless for no reason and

190
00:13:22,680 --> 00:13:24,960
those feelings don't go away.

191
00:13:24,960 --> 00:13:28,680
They may be very angry most of the time and crying a lot or overreacting to

192
00:13:28,680 --> 00:13:30,240
things.

193
00:13:30,240 --> 00:13:33,760
They may have feelings of worthlessness or guilt.

194
00:13:33,760 --> 00:13:37,199
They may feel anxious or worried often.

195
00:13:37,199 --> 00:13:41,439
They may be unable to get over the loss or death of somebody who was

196
00:13:41,439 --> 00:13:43,520
important to them.

197
00:13:43,520 --> 00:13:47,800
They may be extremely fearful or having unexplained fears.

198
00:13:47,800 --> 00:13:51,480
They may be constantly concerned about physical problems or their physical

199
00:13:51,480 --> 00:13:53,160
appearance.

200
00:13:53,160 --> 00:13:58,320
They may be frightened that their mind either is controlled or is out of

201
00:13:58,320 --> 00:14:00,000
control.

202
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:03,680
The other area of concern is when a child or adolescent experiences big

203
00:14:03,680 --> 00:14:08,040
changes like showing declining performance at school or losing interest

204
00:14:08,040 --> 00:14:11,400
in things they once enjoyed or experiencing unexplained changes in

205
00:14:11,400 --> 00:14:13,800
sleeping or eating patterns.

206
00:14:13,800 --> 00:14:18,320
They may avoid friends or family and want to be alone all the time.

207
00:14:18,320 --> 00:14:21,880
They may be daydreaming too much and not completing tasks.

208
00:14:21,880 --> 00:14:25,080
They may be feeling life is too hard to handle.

209
00:14:25,080 --> 00:14:29,080
Now some of the things that I've just said, you're going to look at the

210
00:14:29,080 --> 00:14:31,880
adolescent that you're living with or the ones that you serve as a

211
00:14:31,880 --> 00:14:36,480
scout leader and you're going to say, wow, you know, I see this one

212
00:14:36,480 --> 00:14:38,080
thing happening.

213
00:14:38,080 --> 00:14:42,920
It's really important that we understand that these are not single

214
00:14:42,920 --> 00:14:47,280
indicators of a profound mental health problem.

215
00:14:47,280 --> 00:14:49,600
Sometimes they're an expression of growing up.

216
00:14:49,600 --> 00:14:53,360
But a scout leader who's on their toes is going to watch for these

217
00:14:53,360 --> 00:14:57,680
things and is going to make sure that they bring them to the

218
00:14:57,680 --> 00:15:03,080
attention of the scout's parents if they raise to an inappropriate

219
00:15:03,080 --> 00:15:06,720
level and create a concern.

220
00:15:06,760 --> 00:15:08,320
So what are we going to do?

221
00:15:08,320 --> 00:15:10,120
What are we going to do as scout leaders?

222
00:15:10,120 --> 00:15:13,080
I mean, this is a pretty sobering subject, isn't it?

223
00:15:13,080 --> 00:15:14,960
Well, this is pretty sobering talk, isn't it?

224
00:15:14,960 --> 00:15:17,120
I mean, here we are, we're volunteer scout leaders.

225
00:15:17,120 --> 00:15:18,240
We're not professionals.

226
00:15:18,240 --> 00:15:22,280
We're there to have fun and make life good for the

227
00:15:22,280 --> 00:15:23,880
scouts that we serve.

228
00:15:23,880 --> 00:15:26,040
But we're all going to run into problems.

229
00:15:26,040 --> 00:15:29,200
We're all going to see things that concern us.

230
00:15:29,200 --> 00:15:31,480
Now, what do we do next?

231
00:15:31,480 --> 00:15:33,800
Well, we need to raise those concerns.

232
00:15:33,800 --> 00:15:37,080
We need to be brave enough to talk about them.

233
00:15:37,080 --> 00:15:39,680
And we need to be brave enough to approach a set of parents

234
00:15:39,680 --> 00:15:44,840
about them if they raise a major concern like this.

235
00:15:44,840 --> 00:15:49,720
It could be something that has a broad effect on a young

236
00:15:49,720 --> 00:15:51,440
person's life.

237
00:15:51,440 --> 00:15:54,200
It's definitely something for us to be aware of.

238
00:15:54,200 --> 00:15:57,480
It's a skill set for us to develop so that we know how

239
00:15:57,480 --> 00:15:59,400
to address these things when they do arise.

240
00:16:33,800 --> 00:16:51,400
This is Cliff Jacobson, and you're listening to the

241
00:16:51,400 --> 00:16:54,160
Scoutmaster podcast with Clark Green.

242
00:16:54,160 --> 00:17:05,399
And now here's one of your phone calls.

243
00:17:05,399 --> 00:17:15,280
You too can pick up the phone and give us a call.

244
00:17:15,280 --> 00:17:16,839
Hi, this is Blaise Vitelli.

245
00:17:16,839 --> 00:17:18,920
I'm a Weebles leader who's about to become the

246
00:17:18,920 --> 00:17:21,960
scoutmaster of the newly reborn Troop 560 of

247
00:17:21,960 --> 00:17:23,879
Grantsburg, Wisconsin.

248
00:17:23,920 --> 00:17:26,840
I've truly enjoyed listening to each of your podcasts.

249
00:17:26,840 --> 00:17:29,760
As each topic is brought up, I tended to develop some fear

250
00:17:29,760 --> 00:17:32,480
about a problem I had not really understood.

251
00:17:32,480 --> 00:17:35,440
However, by the end of the podcast, I usually feel that

252
00:17:35,440 --> 00:17:37,760
I'll be able to handle that issue.

253
00:17:37,760 --> 00:17:40,600
Currently, I'm trying to get my Weeble 02s to sign up

254
00:17:40,600 --> 00:17:42,360
for Boy Scout summer camp.

255
00:17:42,360 --> 00:17:44,600
I've run into some resistance from the parents who don't

256
00:17:44,600 --> 00:17:47,280
think their son can be away from mom for a week.

257
00:17:47,280 --> 00:17:49,880
I'd appreciate any advice related to how you

258
00:17:49,880 --> 00:17:52,960
encourage participation in Scout camp, how you deal with

259
00:17:52,960 --> 00:17:56,720
the parents, and how you deal with homesick kids.

260
00:17:56,720 --> 00:17:58,360
Thanks again, bye.

261
00:17:58,360 --> 00:18:00,280
Hey, Blaise, thanks for getting in touch.

262
00:18:00,280 --> 00:18:01,960
I'll tell you, this is something that comes up

263
00:18:01,960 --> 00:18:03,400
every single year.

264
00:18:03,400 --> 00:18:04,480
That's right.

265
00:18:04,480 --> 00:18:06,680
Right around this time of year, we're signing boys up

266
00:18:06,680 --> 00:18:09,360
to go to summer camp, getting them committed to go.

267
00:18:09,360 --> 00:18:11,320
And they'll be these younger guys.

268
00:18:11,320 --> 00:18:14,720
These guys probably just crossed over from Weeblows

269
00:18:14,720 --> 00:18:16,360
and just joined the Troop.

270
00:18:16,360 --> 00:18:19,400
And I'll get a phone call or an email, or I'll have

271
00:18:19,440 --> 00:18:22,920
mom kind of come up to me at a Scout meeting and say,

272
00:18:22,920 --> 00:18:24,680
I want to learn a little bit more about this whole

273
00:18:24,680 --> 00:18:25,480
summer camp thing.

274
00:18:25,480 --> 00:18:29,440
And I can see that their brow is furrowed with worry.

275
00:18:29,440 --> 00:18:33,120
And they're not really worried about the boys.

276
00:18:33,120 --> 00:18:34,440
They say that they are.

277
00:18:34,440 --> 00:18:36,640
But I've learned to tell over the years,

278
00:18:36,640 --> 00:18:39,640
they're more worried about how they're going to take

279
00:18:39,640 --> 00:18:43,680
having their son away for a week of time.

280
00:18:43,680 --> 00:18:47,760
It's usually the first time that a Scout is going

281
00:18:47,800 --> 00:18:50,920
to be away for this long from home.

282
00:18:50,920 --> 00:18:52,320
And moms worry about this.

283
00:18:52,320 --> 00:18:53,600
They really do.

284
00:18:53,600 --> 00:18:55,520
And it's something that we need to get in touch with

285
00:18:55,520 --> 00:18:57,440
if you're not a mom.

286
00:18:57,440 --> 00:19:00,120
You need to know what they're concerned about.

287
00:19:00,120 --> 00:19:02,200
And you need to really address those concerns.

288
00:19:02,200 --> 00:19:05,480
Now listen, I think it's a great thing for boys

289
00:19:05,480 --> 00:19:07,200
to go to camp for a week.

290
00:19:07,200 --> 00:19:09,360
I know that usually they start off being

291
00:19:09,360 --> 00:19:10,200
a little miserable.

292
00:19:10,200 --> 00:19:12,480
Sometimes they get a little homesick.

293
00:19:12,480 --> 00:19:15,480
Sometimes they feel a little troubled

294
00:19:15,480 --> 00:19:16,400
about the experience.

295
00:19:16,400 --> 00:19:17,760
But usually within a couple of days,

296
00:19:17,760 --> 00:19:21,040
everything straightens out and they do just fine.

297
00:19:21,040 --> 00:19:22,920
While mom's sitting at home,

298
00:19:22,920 --> 00:19:25,040
and she's wondering if what he's eating,

299
00:19:25,040 --> 00:19:27,720
if anybody's asking him to take a shower

300
00:19:27,720 --> 00:19:31,600
once in a while, if he's putting on clean clothes,

301
00:19:31,600 --> 00:19:34,600
if he's feeling bad or homesick,

302
00:19:34,600 --> 00:19:37,320
whether she should maybe get in touch with him.

303
00:19:37,320 --> 00:19:38,440
It's a tough thing.

304
00:19:38,440 --> 00:19:41,800
What I recommend is directly addressing this

305
00:19:41,800 --> 00:19:44,200
and making sure that moms understand

306
00:19:44,240 --> 00:19:47,400
that the concerns that they have are important

307
00:19:47,400 --> 00:19:48,880
and that they will be met.

308
00:19:48,880 --> 00:19:51,200
I found a real good article about this

309
00:19:51,200 --> 00:19:53,240
and there's a link to it in the post

310
00:19:53,240 --> 00:19:55,400
that contains this podcast.

311
00:19:55,400 --> 00:19:56,800
And it says this in part.

312
00:19:56,800 --> 00:19:58,200
It says, I found I was uncomfortable

313
00:19:58,200 --> 00:20:00,920
turning over full-time care of my son

314
00:20:00,920 --> 00:20:02,960
to people I didn't really know.

315
00:20:02,960 --> 00:20:05,240
I worried about whether he would like the food.

316
00:20:05,240 --> 00:20:08,520
I wondered who would make sure he had clean clothes,

317
00:20:08,520 --> 00:20:10,880
that he took a shower every day,

318
00:20:10,880 --> 00:20:13,880
what the other boys were going to treat him like,

319
00:20:13,880 --> 00:20:15,600
whether they were going to tease him,

320
00:20:15,600 --> 00:20:17,720
what if he just didn't like camp.

321
00:20:17,720 --> 00:20:19,880
I told him that if he was unhappy,

322
00:20:19,880 --> 00:20:22,760
he could call us and that we'd come and get him

323
00:20:22,760 --> 00:20:25,800
and that I would call him to see how he was doing.

324
00:20:25,800 --> 00:20:27,320
But it turned out that wouldn't work

325
00:20:27,320 --> 00:20:31,280
because the use of telephones was actively discouraged.

326
00:20:32,440 --> 00:20:34,920
She went on to say I actually cried

327
00:20:34,920 --> 00:20:36,520
when he left for camp.

328
00:20:36,520 --> 00:20:38,760
And at home, the house seemed very quiet

329
00:20:38,760 --> 00:20:40,800
and since we couldn't call,

330
00:20:40,800 --> 00:20:42,400
I wrote him lots of letters.

331
00:20:42,400 --> 00:20:45,240
The first letter, we got back sounded miserable.

332
00:20:45,240 --> 00:20:46,680
And I thought about going to get him

333
00:20:46,680 --> 00:20:47,760
and having to bring him home,

334
00:20:47,760 --> 00:20:49,040
but the next letter sounded

335
00:20:49,040 --> 00:20:51,040
like he was really having fun.

336
00:20:51,040 --> 00:20:54,080
It's a tough thing for a mom to do.

337
00:20:54,080 --> 00:20:56,240
Boys, it can be a little tough,

338
00:20:56,240 --> 00:20:57,240
but they'll get over it.

339
00:20:57,240 --> 00:20:58,920
Moms, this is a big deal.

340
00:20:58,920 --> 00:21:00,840
We need to make sure that moms are reassured

341
00:21:00,840 --> 00:21:01,840
about this.

342
00:21:01,840 --> 00:21:03,280
It's very simple.

343
00:21:03,280 --> 00:21:07,600
And you just need to have good direct talk about it

344
00:21:07,600 --> 00:21:09,840
and not be one of those guys that says,

345
00:21:09,879 --> 00:21:11,439
hey, you know,

346
00:21:11,439 --> 00:21:14,720
oh, summer camp's really hard on a first year guy

347
00:21:14,720 --> 00:21:16,480
but they get over it and they get tough

348
00:21:16,480 --> 00:21:18,840
and things like, no, no.

349
00:21:18,840 --> 00:21:20,639
Moms don't wanna hear that.

350
00:21:20,639 --> 00:21:23,959
What they wanna hear is all of their concerns

351
00:21:23,959 --> 00:21:24,800
will be addressed

352
00:21:24,800 --> 00:21:27,560
and that they'll probably make it through the week.

353
00:21:27,560 --> 00:21:30,360
The mom will, you know, as well as the boy.

354
00:21:30,360 --> 00:21:31,800
There's a couple of things that you can do

355
00:21:31,800 --> 00:21:33,520
to make this happen.

356
00:21:33,520 --> 00:21:35,959
I have a couple of go-to people in the troop,

357
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:37,480
go-to moms,

358
00:21:37,480 --> 00:21:41,360
who if I get serious concerns about summer camp,

359
00:21:41,360 --> 00:21:44,280
I will ask the mother who's talking to me

360
00:21:44,280 --> 00:21:45,960
to talk to one of them

361
00:21:45,960 --> 00:21:48,280
and kind of help them through the experience

362
00:21:48,280 --> 00:21:49,160
a little bit.

363
00:21:49,160 --> 00:21:51,240
It's also worth talking about in the materials

364
00:21:51,240 --> 00:21:52,640
that you distribute for summer camp

365
00:21:52,640 --> 00:21:54,280
just as a reassurance

366
00:21:54,280 --> 00:21:55,960
that the boys are gonna be looked after,

367
00:21:55,960 --> 00:21:58,320
that there's medical staff there,

368
00:21:58,320 --> 00:22:00,440
that there's standards for hygiene and food

369
00:22:00,440 --> 00:22:03,880
and that somebody's looking after the boys.

370
00:22:03,880 --> 00:22:07,120
Because if mom's never been to scout camp before,

371
00:22:07,120 --> 00:22:10,080
she has no idea what the experience is gonna be like

372
00:22:10,080 --> 00:22:12,320
and you fear things that you don't know.

373
00:22:12,320 --> 00:22:14,040
So getting to know about it

374
00:22:14,040 --> 00:22:17,240
is a pretty important piece for any mother

375
00:22:17,240 --> 00:22:19,240
who's gonna be sending a younger scout

376
00:22:19,240 --> 00:22:21,240
off to camp for a week.

377
00:22:21,240 --> 00:22:22,920
As far as dealing with boys who get homesick,

378
00:22:22,920 --> 00:22:25,840
well, I've got some references for that too

379
00:22:25,840 --> 00:22:28,880
and I'll have those linked on the blog post

380
00:22:28,880 --> 00:22:31,000
that contains the podcast

381
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:33,080
just to give a very quick review of it.

382
00:22:34,040 --> 00:22:35,800
Homesickness is for real.

383
00:22:35,800 --> 00:22:36,960
It's very treatable

384
00:22:37,920 --> 00:22:40,240
and there are some measures that you can take

385
00:22:40,240 --> 00:22:41,920
before you go to camp to make sure

386
00:22:41,920 --> 00:22:44,680
that it's manageable for all of your scouts.

387
00:22:44,680 --> 00:22:46,480
Blaze, I really appreciate you getting in touch.

388
00:22:46,480 --> 00:22:47,920
It's a great question

389
00:22:47,920 --> 00:22:50,760
and you can call up with your questions

390
00:22:50,760 --> 00:22:53,200
and what I wanna hear this week

391
00:22:53,200 --> 00:22:54,720
is I'd like you to call in

392
00:22:54,720 --> 00:22:57,320
and give me your favorite scout joke

393
00:22:58,160 --> 00:22:59,200
because if you haven't noticed,

394
00:22:59,200 --> 00:23:00,040
I'm kind of running out

395
00:23:00,040 --> 00:23:01,520
and you're gonna learn how to do that in a minute.

396
00:23:32,120 --> 00:23:33,680
Thanks for listening.

397
00:23:33,680 --> 00:23:35,040
Here's how to keep in touch.

398
00:23:35,040 --> 00:23:36,760
You can read the Scoutmaster blog

399
00:23:36,760 --> 00:23:39,879
at scoutmaster.typepad.com.

400
00:23:39,879 --> 00:23:40,879
You can also subscribe

401
00:23:40,879 --> 00:23:43,400
to the Scoutmaster newsletter at the blog.

402
00:23:43,400 --> 00:23:45,200
It's a monthly digest of the blog

403
00:23:45,200 --> 00:23:47,720
and the podcast with some great scouting resources

404
00:23:47,720 --> 00:23:49,560
around a monthly theme.

405
00:23:49,560 --> 00:23:51,120
Every newsletter includes a giveaway

406
00:23:51,120 --> 00:23:53,160
from one of our affiliate sponsors

407
00:23:53,160 --> 00:23:54,680
and this month coming up,

408
00:23:54,680 --> 00:23:57,600
we're going to have a series of products from Elight

409
00:23:57,600 --> 00:24:00,879
and Elight is a electrolyte replacement product

410
00:24:00,920 --> 00:24:03,160
that I'm very excited to tell you about.

411
00:24:09,440 --> 00:24:11,800
You can subscribe to the Scoutmaster podcast on iTunes

412
00:24:11,800 --> 00:24:14,200
and when you do, feel free to leave a comment

413
00:24:14,200 --> 00:24:15,760
or review a rating

414
00:24:15,760 --> 00:24:17,840
and you can also call in and leave a voicemail

415
00:24:17,840 --> 00:24:19,360
and be included in the podcast

416
00:24:19,360 --> 00:24:21,120
just like Blaze was this week.

417
00:24:21,120 --> 00:24:22,080
That's right.

418
00:24:22,080 --> 00:24:27,080
Call in 484-734-0002.

419
00:24:27,840 --> 00:24:28,680
One more time.

420
00:24:28,680 --> 00:24:33,680
484-734-0002.

421
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:36,440
And you can leave a voicemail

422
00:24:36,440 --> 00:24:39,760
that I will then take and include in the podcast.

423
00:24:39,760 --> 00:24:41,360
This week's assignment,

424
00:24:41,360 --> 00:24:44,160
call in and tell me your favorite scouting joke.

425
00:24:44,160 --> 00:24:46,080
That's right, I'm running out.

426
00:24:46,080 --> 00:24:47,080
Give me some help.

427
00:24:54,080 --> 00:24:56,960
You can follow us at Scoutmaster blog on Facebook

428
00:24:56,960 --> 00:24:59,000
and ScoutmasterCG on Twitter

429
00:24:59,000 --> 00:25:01,000
and you can email me, Clark Green,

430
00:25:01,000 --> 00:25:02,560
with your comments and questions

431
00:25:02,560 --> 00:25:05,480
at clarkgreen at gmail.com.

432
00:25:05,480 --> 00:25:10,480
That's C-L-A-R-K-E-G-R-E-E-N at gmail.com.

433
00:25:16,360 --> 00:25:17,400
Hey, it's time to remind you

434
00:25:17,400 --> 00:25:19,960
that the Scoutmaster blog and the Scoutmaster podcast

435
00:25:19,960 --> 00:25:21,960
are not official publications of the Boy Scouts

436
00:25:21,960 --> 00:25:24,440
of America, nor are they endorsed or sponsored

437
00:25:24,440 --> 00:25:26,480
by the Boy Scouts of America.

438
00:25:26,480 --> 00:25:28,080
Hey, it's just me.

439
00:25:28,080 --> 00:25:29,960
I have a computer and a microphone.

440
00:25:29,960 --> 00:25:31,160
I like to talk into it

441
00:25:32,360 --> 00:25:34,680
and hopefully lend a hand to Scout leaders

442
00:25:34,680 --> 00:25:37,120
and maybe have a little bit of fun along the way.

443
00:25:39,840 --> 00:25:41,240
Before we leave you,

444
00:25:41,240 --> 00:25:44,800
let's hear from our founder, Sir Robert Baden Powell.

445
00:25:44,800 --> 00:25:46,040
Sir Robert?

446
00:25:46,040 --> 00:25:48,280
Good luck to you and good camping.

447
00:25:49,320 --> 00:25:50,840
Why thank you, sir.

448
00:25:50,840 --> 00:25:51,800
Until next time.

